@startbpmn BP15_ReviewArchivedMaterial
lang en

output L1 "01_detailed/BP15_ReviewArchivedMaterial.bpmn"
output L2 "02_detailed/BP15_ReviewArchivedMaterial.bpmn"

' Provenance (never in a drawn note, lint rule 5):
'   - archived items are retained, hidden from the working view, read by an explicit act: BR-26
'   - an archived item cannot be edited, any archived item: BR-05, SA 2026-10-10
'   - unarchive must leave the overall state consistent and is rejected whenever it cannot: BR-25
'   - a partial unarchive can never be consistent and is refused in either direction: BR-25
'   - the bundle comes back in its prior state and prior position: BR-25
'   - an archived item's position is never occupied while it is away: BR-25
'   - where the bundle cannot be reassembled the item stays archived: BR-25
'   - five archivable kinds: idea, goal, progress measure with its check-in history, project with
'     its activities and tasks, task: BR-23, SA 2026-10-16
'   - an archived goal carries its check-out record, and unarchiving never revokes it: BR-30
'   - a goal's measures and their check-in history come back with it, and a measure archived on its
'     own brings its own history: BR-112, BR-15, SA 2026-10-16
'   - unarchiving offers the user nothing to choose and destroys nothing, so it discloses nothing
'     and is not confirmed: the one request brings the bundle back where that is possible and says
'     why it cannot where it is not, both outcomes leaving by one report. Call site BP15.02 (See
'     what unarchiving will bring back) is RETIRED, its id never reused: BR-25, SA 2026-10-16,
'     ADR-066
'   - NEITHER LOOKING NOR NARROWING IS AN ACT. The archive holds one view per kind, reached from the
'     tab that owns that kind, so there is no kind to switch inside it; what the view shows is in
'     play and frozen, so looking at an item asks the system for nothing and decides nothing; and
'     filtering or sorting the list is the client's own business, remembered interface state rather
'     than anything the system is asked for. So this process draws the TWO acts an archived item
'     takes - bring it back, or destroy it - and nothing else: BR-26, BR-92, DRW-14, section 7,
'     SA 2026-10-18 ("if there would be filtering, that would be up to the client" ... "'Read the
'     item as it stands' is not a genuine activity")
'   - an archived item is frozen: reading one changes nothing, and changing one means bringing it
'     back first, which is why no editing act is drawn here: BR-05
'   - deleting an archived item IS the item's own deletion, so it carries the selection step its
'     live deletion carries: for a kind whose cascade may be declined there is a combination to
'     pick, and for a leaf there is exactly one: BR-12, BR-19, DRW-17, DRW-19
'   - NOTHING IS FETCHED HERE. Every record is loaded once, at start-up (BP01), so a step that only
'     went to the store is gone, and one that selects or qualifies what the act works on says so
'     instead of saying read. What a chain still asks the system for is what it COMPUTES, which is
'     business and stays out of the client. When data is read is a detail design decision:
'     BR-128, DRW-14, SA 2026-10-17 ("when we will read data is not a business concern, but a
'     detail design decision ... I rule that this will be done once at startup")
'
' Scope: this process reads the archive, brings things back, and destroys what is to be destroyed
' out of it. Putting items there is each entity's own process; taking one out for good is THIS
' process's, because the owning processes all read live items only and the archive is the one
' surface the item is in front of the user at all: BR-05, BR-26, G-1, SA 2026-10-16 ("when we
' identify a concrete issue, we fix it, even if I will review the affected BP only in the
' future").
'
' One delete serves all five kinds, like the read and the unarchive: the kind is the request's
' payload and what the deletion reaches is the item's OWN rule, applied where it reaches and never
' redrawn here (BR-12, DRW-19). Three of the five kinds own something, so the drawing carries the
' disclose-then-approve shape of every other destructive act; for a leaf - a task, a measure - the
' disclosure is simply empty, which is the one case DRW-28 cannot express in a single branch
' without dispatching on the kind, and dispatching on the kind is a permutation, not a gateway.
'
' Call-site collapsing: the five archivable kinds are not five operations. Unarchiving is one
' request whose consequences differ by what the item carries rather than by which operation is
' called, and deleting is the same. This is why the eight candidates in Intent section 6 draw as
' three.

pool "User" {
  start "Something to look up, or something to bring back"
  :Work the archive;
  rule BR-118

  if "What needs doing?" then "drop one for good"
    :Delete an archived item;
    rule BR-05
    send "See what deleting the archived item takes" to delImpactIn
    receive "What the deletion will take" as delImpact
    if "Approve what the deletion takes?" rule BR-12 then "approve"
      :Select a valid cascading option;
      rule BR-19
      :Confirm the deletion;
      send "Delete an archived item" to deleteIn
      receive "The deletion outcome" as deleted
      note "What the item owns goes with it and what hangs on it is the cascade its own rule gives, exactly as it would for a live item. A leaf has nothing to disclose, so the disclosure is empty and there is nothing to weigh."
    else "reject"
      end "Deletion abandoned"
    endif
    end "Item destroyed"

  elseif "What needs doing?" then "bring one back"
    :Unarchive an item;
    send "Unarchive an item" to unarchiveIn
    receive "What came back, or why it cannot" as unarchived
    note "The whole bundle comes back as it went: no part of it can be picked and nothing is re-parented, relocated or approximated. A member deleted out of the archive, or a surrounding that is now gone, leaves it unreassemblable, and then the item stays archived."
    end "Item brought back"
  endif
}

system pool "Bearing" {
  messageStart "Deletion impact requested" as delImpactIn
  call "BP15.04_ReadTheArchivedDeletionImpact" "See what deleting the archived item takes" {
    :Report what the deletion will take, and what of it can be declined;
    rule BR-12
    rule BR-19
    rule BR-117
  }
  send "What the deletion will take" to delImpact
  note "The item's own rule governs what the deletion reaches, as it does for a live one. Where the item owns nothing the answer is empty."
  end "Deletion impact answered"

  messageStart "Deletion requested" as deleteIn
  call "BP15.05_DeleteArchivedItem" "Delete an archived item" {
    :Delete confirmed items;
    rule BR-12
    rule BR-117
    :Report the outcome;
  }
  send "The deletion outcome" to deleted
  note "Whatever bundle needed the deleted item can no longer be reassembled, so an unarchive that wanted it is refused afterwards and that item stays archived."
  end "Deletion answered"

  messageStart "Unarchive requested" as unarchiveIn
  call "BP15.03_UnarchiveItem" "Unarchive an item" {
    if "Can the bundle come back whole?" rule BR-25 then "yes"
      :Return the bundle to its prior state and position;
      rule BR-25
      goto report
    else "no"
      goto report
    endif
    task "Report the outcome" as report
  }
  send "What came back, or why it cannot" to unarchived
  note "A project brings its activities and their tasks; a goal brings its measures and their check-in history; a measure brings its own, and a goal's check-out record is untouched. An archived item's position is never occupied while it is away, so the bundle comes back to it. One answer carries both outcomes."
  end "Unarchive answered"
}

@endbpmn
